WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.877
What a framework actually gives you.

2
00:00:02.157 --> 00:00:07.363
You have now written an agent loop by hand twice: in Studio 1 and in this week’s lab.

3
00:00:07.643 --> 00:00:13.488
It is a few dozen lines: call the model, run the tools, append the results, check the limits, repeat.

4
00:00:13.768 --> 00:00:15.304
So what do frameworks add?

5
00:00:15.584 --> 00:00:26.656
Roughly five things, in increasing order of value for long tasks: Tool plumbing: generating schemas from functions, parsing calls, wrapping exceptions as error results.

6
00:00:26.936 --> 00:00:29.027
The loop itself, with limits and hooks.

7
00:00:29.307 --> 00:00:33.765
State and persistence: saving a run so it can pause, crash, and resume.

8
00:00:34.045 --> 00:00:39.144
Observability: traces of every model and tool call, often in a hosted viewer.

9
00:00:39.424 --> 00:00:44.608
Hosting: a place for the agent and its tools to run, such as a sandbox per session.

10
00:00:44.888 --> 00:00:55.704
What they cost: another abstraction between you and the model, defaults you did not choose, a dependency that changes on its own schedule, and sometimes a tie to one provider.

11
00:00:55.984 --> 00:01:10.427
Anthropic’s “Building effective agents” puts the risk plainly: frameworks can add layers of abstraction that obscure the underlying prompts and responses and make systems harder to debug, and they can tempt you into complexity you don’t need.

12
00:01:10.707 --> 00:01:21.715
Their advice is to start with the LLM API directly, since many patterns take only a few lines of code, and if you do adopt a framework, to understand the code underneath it.

13
00:01:21.995 --> 00:01:26.112
This lesson uses only official documentation for each option.

14
00:01:26.392 --> 00:01:32.984
Frameworks change quickly, so treat the specifics as true at the time of writing and check the docs before you commit.
